  "summary": "Recent preprints present analytical and frequency-dependent theory of light-induced spin and magnetization responses in altermagnets and graphene.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread across these preprints is the optical generation of spin or magnetization responses through interband physics. The theory of the frequency-dependent spin Edelstein response in a 2D d-wave altermagnet with Rashba coupling links the response to interband quantities and the quantum metric. In intrinsic graphene, an analytical quantum treatment of the inverse Faraday effect attributes helicity-dependent orbital magnetization to interband coherence. What is new in both cases is the explicit microscopic role of interband quantities, specifically quantum metric and coherence, in setting light-induced spin and magnetization. The third preprint, on magnetoplasmons coupled to higher cyclotron harmonics under spatially inhomogeneous fields in 2D electron systems, addresses a different mechanism and is best read as a neighboring theoretical study of 2D electron responses rather than as part of the same optical-spin thread.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-18"
}
